  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 1, 'The Discovery of Penicillin', '[{"label":null,"text":"In September 1928, the Scottish bacteriologist Alexander Fleming returned from a summer holiday to his laboratory at St Mary’s Hospital in London to find something unusual. A petri dish in which he had been culturing Staphylococcus bacteria had become contaminated with a stray mould, and Fleming noticed that the area immediately surrounding the mould was completely free of bacterial growth. Curious, he identified the mould as belonging to the genus Penicillium, specifically Penicillium notatum, and correctly deduced that it was producing a substance capable of killing the surrounding bacteria — a substance he would later name penicillin."},{"label":null,"text":"Fleming published his findings in 1929, but the discovery attracted remarkably little attention from the wider scientific community. He struggled to purify the compound into a stable form suitable for treating infections, and without a reliable method of production, he found it difficult to convince others of its therapeutic potential. For roughly a decade, research into penicillin as a practical medicine largely stalled."},{"label":null,"text":"It was not until the late 1930s that the discovery was revived, this time at Oxford University, by the Australian-born pathologist Howard Florey and the German-born biochemist Ernst Chain. Working together, the pair developed methods to extract and purify penicillin in sufficient quantity for proper testing. In 1941, they conducted the first human trial on a policeman suffering from a severe infection. His condition improved dramatically within days, but the limited supply of penicillin ran out before treatment could be completed, and he later died — a tragic outcome that nonetheless offered clear proof of the drug’s remarkable potential."},{"label":null,"text":"The outbreak of the Second World War created an urgent need for an effective treatment for infected wounds, and British and American governments, working alongside pharmaceutical companies, collaborated to scale up production using newly developed deep-fermentation techniques. By 1944, in time for the D-Day landings, penicillin was being produced in sufficient quantities to treat large numbers of wounded soldiers, saving countless lives that would previously have been lost to infection."},{"label":null,"text":"In recognition of this transformative achievement, Fleming, Florey and Chain were jointly awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1945. Their work ushered in what is often called the antibiotic era, making it possible to treat bacterial infections that had previously been fatal and paving the way for complex surgery and treatments such as chemotherapy, which would otherwise carry an unacceptably high risk of infection."},{"label":null,"text":"Today, however, the very success of antibiotics like penicillin has given rise to a serious new concern. Decades of overuse and misuse have contributed to the emergence of antibiotic-resistant bacteria, and organisations such as the World Health Organization have warned of the possibility of a so-called post-antibiotic era if resistance continues to spread unchecked. Researchers around the world are now racing to discover entirely new classes of antibiotics as existing ones gradually lose their effectiveness."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 2, 'Coral Reefs Under Threat', '[{"label":"Paragraph A","text":"Although coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, they are estimated to support roughly a quarter of all known marine species, earning them the popular nickname “rainforests of the sea”. A reef is built not by a single organism but by vast colonies of tiny animals called polyps, which slowly secrete calcium carbonate skeletons that, over hundreds or even thousands of years, accumulate into the elaborate structures divers recognise today."},{"label":"Paragraph B","text":"The vivid colours associated with healthy reefs come from a remarkable partnership between coral polyps and microscopic algae called zooxanthellae, which live within the polyps’ tissues. Through photosynthesis, these algae generate food and energy that the coral relies on to survive, while in return the coral provides the algae with shelter and the compounds they need to photosynthesise effectively. Neither partner thrives without the other."},{"label":"Paragraph C","text":"This delicate partnership is highly sensitive to temperature. When surrounding water becomes even slightly warmer than usual for a sustained period, stressed coral polyps expel their zooxanthellae, causing the coral to turn a stark white in a process known as bleaching. Bleached coral is not immediately dead, but it is severely weakened and considerably more vulnerable to disease and starvation. As ocean temperatures continue to rise as a result of climate change, bleaching events have become alarmingly more frequent."},{"label":"Paragraph D","text":"A second, less visible threat comes from ocean acidification. Oceans absorb a significant proportion of the carbon dioxide released into the atmosphere, and this process forms carbonic acid, gradually lowering the water’s pH. In more acidic conditions, coral finds it considerably harder to build and maintain its calcium carbonate skeleton, which slows growth and weakens existing reef structures."},{"label":"Paragraph E","text":"Beyond these global pressures, reefs also face a range of more localised threats. Agricultural runoff introduces excess nutrients and pollutants into coastal waters, overfishing disrupts the delicate balance of reef ecosystems, and unsustainable tourism practices, including careless anchoring, can cause direct physical damage to fragile coral structures. Coastal development can also increase sedimentation, clouding the water and blocking the sunlight that polyps and their algal partners depend on."},{"label":"Paragraph F","text":"In response, marine protected areas have been established in numerous regions, restricting fishing and anchoring in particularly vulnerable zones. Coral restoration projects, in which fragments are grown in nurseries before being transplanted onto damaged reefs, are also becoming increasingly common, and some researchers are experimenting with breeding more heat-resistant coral strains. Scientists are quick to caution, however, that local conservation efforts alone cannot fully offset the impact of global warming, meaning that meaningful reef protection ultimately depends on both local action and a genuine reduction in global emissions."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 3, 'The Future of Work: Automation and Employment', '[{"label":null,"text":"The question of whether machines will ultimately replace human workers is far from new, but it has taken on fresh urgency amid recent advances in artificial intelligence and robotics. Historically, waves of technological change — from the mechanisation of agriculture to the introduction of assembly-line robots — have eliminated certain categories of work while simultaneously creating others. The central question today is whether the current wave is fundamentally different, given that it increasingly affects cognitive tasks, not merely manual ones."},{"label":null,"text":"Optimists point to a reassuring historical pattern: despite repeated predictions of mass unemployment following previous waves of automation, structural unemployment has not risen over the long term, and new industries and job categories have consistently emerged to absorb displaced workers. Many economists regard this pattern as grounds for cautious confidence about the future."},{"label":null,"text":"There is, however, reason for caution. The present wave of change may genuinely be different, both because it is unfolding considerably faster than previous transitions, leaving workers and institutions with less time to adapt, and because the range of tasks now capable of being automated spans both manual and white-collar professions simultaneously, unlike earlier waves that were largely confined to manual labour."},{"label":null,"text":"Certain sectors appear particularly exposed. Roles involving routine, predictable tasks — including basic data entry, simple bookkeeping, some forms of legal document review, and increasingly, transportation, through the gradual development of self-driving vehicles — are considered especially vulnerable. By contrast, sectors that depend heavily on complex interpersonal skills, creativity or unpredictable physical dexterity, such as healthcare, skilled trades and the creative industries, appear comparatively insulated from automation, at least for the foreseeable future."},{"label":null,"text":"In my view, the policy response to automation matters considerably more than the underlying technology itself. Sensible measures would include sustained investment in retraining programmes, the development of portable benefits that are not tied to a single employer, and a broader rethinking of education systems to emphasise adaptability over narrow technical training. Societies that prepare proactively for this transition are likely to fare far better than those that wait passively for disruption to occur before responding."}]'::jsonb);

