  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 1, 'The Silk Road: Trade Across Continents', '[{"label":null,"text":"Contrary to what its name suggests, the Silk Road was never a single continuous road. Rather, it was a vast network of overland trade routes connecting China with Central Asia, the Middle East and, eventually, Europe, first established during the Han Dynasty in roughly the second century BCE. The evocative name itself is considerably more recent than the routes it describes: it was coined only in the nineteenth century by a German geographer named Richthofen, long after the network’s peak."},{"label":null,"text":"Silk was the commodity that gave the network its name, and for centuries the secret of its manufacture was closely guarded within China. Yet silk was far from the only good in transit: traders also carried spices, precious stones, glassware and, later, paper and gunpowder. Goods rarely travelled the entire route with a single merchant; instead, they typically passed through the hands of numerous middlemen, changing caravans at bustling oasis towns along the way."},{"label":null,"text":"The Silk Road’s significance extended well beyond commerce. It served as a conduit for the exchange of religion — Buddhism, for instance, spread from India into China largely along these trade routes — as well as technology, including the westward spread of papermaking, and ideas more broadly. Cities such as Samarkand and Kashgar flourished as a result, becoming prosperous, multicultural hubs where merchants, scholars and travellers from vastly different backgrounds regularly crossed paths."},{"label":null,"text":"Alongside the overland network, a maritime Silk Route also existed, connecting Chinese ports to Southeast Asia, India and eventually East Africa and the Arabian Peninsula. This sea-based trade became especially significant during the Tang and Song dynasties, as advances in shipbuilding and navigation made longer sea voyages considerably more practical and profitable."},{"label":null,"text":"The overland routes gradually declined in importance after the fifteenth century, partly as a result of European voyages of exploration, which sought a direct sea route to Asia that would bypass Central Asian middlemen entirely. Ottoman control over several key land routes further increased costs and risk for overland traders, accelerating the shift towards maritime trade."},{"label":null,"text":"The term has, however, experienced something of a revival in recent years. China’s Belt and Road Initiative, launched in 2013, explicitly invokes the historic Silk Road as inspiration for a vast programme of infrastructure investment stretching across Asia, Africa and Europe."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 2, 'Renewable Energy: Solar and Wind Power', '[{"label":"Paragraph A","text":"Driven by mounting concern over climate change and steadily falling technology costs, the world has undergone a marked shift towards renewable sources of electricity over the past two decades. Solar and wind power, once dismissed as niche and prohibitively expensive, are now among the fastest-growing sources of electricity generation globally, and in many markets they have become genuinely cost-competitive with fossil fuels."},{"label":"Paragraph B","text":"Solar electricity is generated using photovoltaic, or PV, cells, which convert sunlight directly into electricity through semiconductor materials, typically silicon. The cost of PV panels has fallen by more than eighty per cent over the past decade alone, driven by improvements in manufacturing scale and underlying technology. While solar remains especially effective in sunny regions, efficiency gains mean it is now a viable option even in comparatively cloudy northern climates."},{"label":"Paragraph C","text":"Wind power, meanwhile, relies on turbines that convert the kinetic energy of moving air into electricity through rotating blades connected to a generator. Onshore wind is a mature and relatively inexpensive technology, while offshore wind, though considerably more expensive to install, benefits from stronger and more consistent winds at sea. Offshore wind capacity has grown particularly rapidly in parts of Europe in recent years."},{"label":"Paragraph D","text":"Both solar and wind share an inherent limitation: unlike traditional fossil fuel plants, which can generate electricity on demand, they are inherently variable, since the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow. This intermittency is increasingly addressed through a combination of battery storage, interconnection across regional electricity grids, and the retention of some flexible backup generation for periods of low output."},{"label":"Paragraph E","text":"The renewable sector now employs millions of people worldwide, and falling costs mean that in many markets, building new solar or wind capacity is now cheaper than constructing a new fossil fuel plant. This transition is not without complications, however, as it also requires the retraining of workers whose livelihoods have traditionally depended on declining fossil fuel industries."},{"label":"Paragraph F","text":"Despite falling generation costs, several obstacles remain. Lengthy permitting delays, insufficient transmission infrastructure to carry electricity from often-remote generation sites to distant urban demand centres, and the continued need for investment in storage technology all pose significant challenges. Increasingly, researchers and policymakers agree that infrastructure investment is now just as important as improvements in generation technology itself."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 3, 'Child Development and Language Acquisition', '[{"label":null,"text":"Human infants acquire language with astonishing speed, progressing from producing no words at all to forming complex, grammatically structured sentences within just a few years, and typically without any formal instruction. This process remains one of the most intensively studied phenomena in cognitive science, and a central debate concerns just how much of this remarkable capacity is innate, as opposed to learned entirely from the surrounding environment."},{"label":null,"text":"One influential position, closely associated with the linguist Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans are born with an innate language faculty, sometimes referred to as universal grammar: a set of grammatical principles common to all human languages that allows a child to acquire whichever language they happen to be exposed to remarkably quickly, despite input that is often limited and imperfect. This has become known as the poverty of stimulus argument."},{"label":null,"text":"An alternative, usage-based view argues that language acquisition can instead be explained through general cognitive and learning mechanisms — pattern recognition, statistical learning and imitation — without needing to assume any dedicated, language-specific innate module. Proponents of this view point to substantial evidence that infants are highly sensitive to statistical regularities in the speech they hear from a very early age."},{"label":null,"text":"In my view, this debate is often presented as more binary than the evidence genuinely supports. Converging research increasingly suggests that language acquisition likely depends on a combination of domain-general learning mechanisms and certain innate biases or predispositions specific to language, rather than either a purely innate grammar or purely general-purpose learning acting alone. Most contemporary researchers now occupy something of a middle ground between the two original positions."},{"label":null,"text":"A related but distinct idea is the critical period hypothesis, which proposes that there exists an optimal window, roughly before the onset of puberty, during which language acquisition occurs most readily. Evidence drawn from rare cases of severely language-deprived children, as well as studies of adult second-language learners, suggests that achieving native-like fluency becomes considerably harder, though not strictly impossible, once this window has closed. The precise boundaries of this critical period, however, remain a subject of ongoing debate among researchers."}]'::jsonb);

