  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 1, 'The Mystery of Bird Migration', '[{"label":null,"text":"Every year, billions of birds undertake extraordinary journeys spanning thousands of kilometres between their breeding grounds and their non-breeding grounds. The Arctic tern holds the remarkable record for the longest known migration of any animal species, travelling from its Arctic breeding grounds to Antarctic waters and back again annually — a round trip of some seventy thousand kilometres."},{"label":null,"text":"Exactly how birds navigate across such vast distances puzzled scientists for centuries. Research now shows that birds rely on multiple overlapping cues: the position of the sun during the day, patterns of stars at night, and, most remarkably, an ability to detect the Earth’s magnetic field, a sense known as magnetoreception, thought to depend on specialised proteins located in the eye that are sensitive to magnetic fields."},{"label":null,"text":"The timing of migration is triggered primarily by changes in day length, or photoperiod, which in turn affects hormone levels. Some species also respond to temperature changes and food availability, though photoperiod is generally considered the most reliable and consistent cue, since, unlike the weather, it does not vary unpredictably from one year to the next."},{"label":null,"text":"Migration is an extraordinarily risky undertaking. Birds cross oceans, deserts and mountain ranges with little or no opportunity to rest or refuel along the way. It is estimated that a substantial proportion of migratory birds, particularly first-year juveniles undertaking their first migration without experienced adults to guide them, do not survive the journey."},{"label":null,"text":"Human activity has introduced further threats. Light pollution from cities disorients night-migrating birds, frequently causing fatal collisions with buildings, while habitat loss at stopover sites, where birds rest and refuel midway through their journey, can be every bit as damaging as the loss of breeding habitat itself. A bird unable to find sufficient food at a critical stopover site may simply lack the energy reserves needed to complete its journey."},{"label":null,"text":"In response, international agreements now protect networks of critical stopover sites across multiple countries, in recognition of the fact that migratory birds pay no attention to national borders. “Lights out” campaigns, which encourage cities to dim non-essential lighting during peak migration seasons, have shown measurable reductions in bird collision deaths wherever they have been adopted."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 2, 'Urbanisation and Public Health', '[{"label":"Paragraph A","text":"More than half of the world’s population now lives in urban areas, and this proportion is expected to keep rising in the decades ahead. Cities have historically offered better access to formal healthcare and sanitation infrastructure than rural areas, contributing to measurable improvements in public health over time. At the same time, urbanisation introduces a distinct set of new health challenges of its own."},{"label":"Paragraph B","text":"Vehicle emissions and concentrated industrial activity mean that air pollution levels in many major cities frequently exceed World Health Organization guidelines. This is linked to a range of respiratory and cardiovascular problems, and poorer neighbourhoods, which are often located closer to industrial zones and busy roads, tend to be disproportionately affected."},{"label":"Paragraph C","text":"Urban design has historically prioritised cars over pedestrians and cyclists in many cities, contributing to increasingly sedentary lifestyles. Easy access to processed, calorie-dense food, combined with reduced everyday physical activity, has been linked to rising rates of obesity among urban populations worldwide."},{"label":"Paragraph D","text":"Higher population density, noise and the generally faster pace of urban life have been associated with elevated rates of certain mental health conditions, including anxiety and mood disorders. However, cities also offer greater access to specialised mental health services and more diverse social support networks, which complicates any simple conclusion about whether city living is, on balance, harmful to mental wellbeing."},{"label":"Paragraph E","text":"High population density in cities facilitates the rapid transmission of infectious disease, a fact demonstrated clearly during the recent global pandemic. Overcrowded informal settlements with inadequate sanitation infrastructure are particularly vulnerable to outbreaks of water-borne and other communicable diseases."},{"label":"Paragraph F","text":"There is growing recognition that thoughtful urban design can directly promote better health outcomes. Green spaces have been shown to reduce stress and encourage physical activity, investment in pedestrian and cycling infrastructure can reduce both pollution and sedentary behaviour, and mixed-use zoning, which places housing closer to everyday amenities, reduces reliance on cars. The so-called “healthy city” movement now influences planning policy in a growing number of municipalities worldwide."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 3, 'The Psychology of Decision-Making', '[{"label":null,"text":"Traditional economic models long assumed that human beings make decisions through careful, rational cost-benefit analysis, weighing all available information in order to select the objectively best option available — an approach often referred to as the rational actor model. Decades of research in behavioural psychology and economics, however, have revealed that actual human decision-making deviates from this ideal in predictable, systematic ways."},{"label":null,"text":"These systematic patterns of deviation are known as cognitive biases. Examples include confirmation bias, the tendency to favour information that confirms existing beliefs while discounting contradictory evidence; anchoring bias, an excessive reliance on the first piece of information encountered when making subsequent judgements; and loss aversion, the tendency to feel losses more intensely than equivalent gains, first documented in detail by the psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky."},{"label":null,"text":"An influential framework known as dual-process theory proposes two distinct systems of thought. System 1 is fast, automatic and intuitive, operating largely below the level of conscious awareness, while System 2 is slower, more deliberate and considerably more effortful, and is typically engaged only for complex reasoning tasks. Most everyday decisions rely heavily on System 1, which is generally efficient but correspondingly more susceptible to the biases described above."},{"label":null,"text":"Critics sometimes portray these biases as evidence that human decision-making is fundamentally flawed or irrational. This framing, while understandable, somewhat misses the point. Many of these apparent biases likely represent efficient heuristics that served our ancestors well in the environments in which human cognition originally evolved, even if they occasionally produce suboptimal outcomes in the more complex, information-rich environment of modern life."},{"label":null,"text":"This understanding of cognitive bias has, in turn, influenced so-called nudge theory, in which choice architecture is subtly adjusted to encourage better decisions without restricting anyone’s freedom of choice — automatic enrolment in a retirement savings scheme, which employees remain free to opt out of, is a frequently cited example. The approach has since been adopted by numerous governments and organisations worldwide, though it remains a subject of ongoing ethical debate concerning the boundary between helpful nudging and outright manipulation."}]'::jsonb);

