  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 1, 'The Invention of the Printing Press', '[{"label":null,"text":"Before the mid-fifteenth century, books circulating in Europe were produced almost entirely by hand, typically copied out laboriously by scribes, often working in monasteries. This process was slow and expensive, meaning that books remained rare and costly objects, and literacy was largely confined to the clergy and a small, wealthy elite. In parts of Asia, by contrast, woodblock printing had already been in use for centuries, though the technique had not become widespread in Europe."},{"label":null,"text":"Johannes Gutenberg’s crucial innovation, developed in the German city of Mainz in the 1440s, was not printing itself but movable metal type: individual, reusable letter blocks that could be arranged and rearranged to compose different texts. Combined with oil-based ink and a printing press adapted from an existing wine press design, this innovation dramatically increased the speed, and correspondingly reduced the cost, of producing books compared with hand-copying."},{"label":null,"text":"Gutenberg’s famous Bible, completed around 1455 and often referred to as the 42-line Bible on account of the number of lines per page, is widely considered the first major book printed in Europe using movable type. Despite the revolutionary nature of his invention, Gutenberg himself did not profit greatly from it: he became embroiled in a financial dispute with his investor, Johann Fust, and ultimately lost control of his own printing workshop and equipment."},{"label":null,"text":"The technology spread with striking speed across Europe in the decades that followed. By 1500, printing presses were operating in more than two hundred European cities, having collectively produced an estimated twenty million books — a remarkable figure, given that books had been a genuinely rare commodity only a few decades earlier."},{"label":null,"text":"The consequences extended far beyond simple convenience. Wider availability of books contributed directly to rising literacy rates, to a gradual standardisation of language, as printed texts tended to favour certain dialects over regional variation, and to the remarkably rapid spread of new ideas. Historians frequently credit the printing press with playing a crucial role in enabling the spread of Renaissance humanism, the Protestant Reformation — Martin Luther’s writings were disseminated widely through print — and, eventually, the Scientific Revolution."},{"label":null,"text":"The printing press is regularly ranked among the most significant inventions in human history for its role in democratising access to information. Some historians draw direct comparisons to the internet’s more recent impact on the spread of information, though most are careful to caution that such comparisons have their limits, given the vastly different technological and social contexts involved."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 2, 'Global Water Scarcity', '[{"label":"Paragraph A","text":"Although the Earth’s surface is mostly covered by water, less than one per cent of the world’s water is readily accessible fresh water suitable for human use; the overwhelming majority is either seawater or locked away in ice caps and glaciers. Billions of people currently experience some form of water scarcity for at least part of the year, and this number is projected to grow further as a result of population growth, climate change, and rising demand from agriculture and industry."},{"label":"Paragraph B","text":"It is worth distinguishing between two different types of scarcity. Physical scarcity occurs when a region simply does not have enough water resources to meet demand, as is the case in many arid regions. Economic scarcity, by contrast, occurs when adequate water resources exist, but a lack of infrastructure or investment prevents people from actually accessing them. Much of sub-Saharan Africa, despite reasonably adequate rainfall in parts of the region, is frequently cited as an example of economic rather than physical scarcity."},{"label":"Paragraph C","text":"Agriculture accounts for roughly seventy per cent of global freshwater withdrawal, by far the largest single use of fresh water worldwide. Inefficient irrigation techniques in many regions waste a substantial proportion of this water before it ever reaches crops. The adoption of more efficient methods, such as drip irrigation and precision agriculture, can dramatically reduce agricultural water consumption without necessarily reducing crop yields."},{"label":"Paragraph D","text":"Climate change is compounding the problem considerably. It is altering precipitation patterns, causing more frequent and severe droughts in some regions while, somewhat paradoxically, increasing flooding risk in others. Glaciers and mountain snowpack, which have traditionally provided a steady water supply to major rivers through gradual seasonal melt, are shrinking in many parts of the world, threatening the long-term reliability of water supply for populations living downstream."},{"label":"Paragraph E","text":"Many regions rely heavily on groundwater stored in underground aquifers, which in numerous cases are being extracted considerably faster than they can naturally replenish. This is particularly concerning because, unlike surface water, aquifer depletion can be effectively irreversible, or may take many centuries to recover, since most aquifers were filled gradually over geological timescales. Several major agricultural regions around the world are now facing serious long-term aquifer depletion."},{"label":"Paragraph F","text":"A range of responses is being pursued, including investment in water-efficient technology, wastewater recycling and treatment for reuse, and desalination technology, which, though energy-intensive and historically expensive, is becoming increasingly viable as costs continue to fall. Improved water pricing structures that better reflect true scarcity value, alongside cross-border cooperation agreements covering shared river basins and aquifers, are also being pursued. Experts consistently stress that no single solution alone will be sufficient; a combination of technological, policy and behavioural change will be required."}]'::jsonb);
  insert into reading_passages (lesson_id, passage_number, title, paragraphs) values (v_lesson, 3, 'Space Tourism: Opportunities and Challenges', '[{"label":null,"text":"Once the exclusive domain of government-employed astronauts, space travel has begun opening up to paying private citizens over the past two decades, driven largely by the emergence of private spaceflight companies developing reusable rocket technology, which has substantially reduced launch costs compared with traditional government space programmes."},{"label":null,"text":"Current forms of space tourism vary considerably. Suborbital flights, which offer a few brief minutes of weightlessness and a view of the Earth’s curvature without reaching full orbital velocity, are offered by some companies at a relatively lower cost, though still expensive by any ordinary standard. Orbital flights, which involve days spent aboard a spacecraft actually orbiting the Earth, are offered by other providers at dramatically higher price points, and have historically been accessible only to extremely wealthy individuals."},{"label":null,"text":"Proponents argue that the space tourism industry could eventually help fund broader space exploration and technology development, and that an expanding pool of people who have personally experienced spaceflight may increase public support and investment in space programmes more generally. Some astronauts report a profound shift in perspective after seeing the Earth from space, sometimes referred to as the “overview effect”."},{"label":null,"text":"Critics, meanwhile, raise several concerns. These include the environmental impact of rocket launches, whose carbon emissions and broader atmospheric effects are still being studied and are not yet fully understood; the inherent safety risks of spaceflight, even with modern technology; and a broader question of resource allocation, since critics argue that the vast sums spent enabling wealthy individuals to enjoy brief recreational spaceflight experiences could arguably be directed towards more pressing problems here on Earth."},{"label":null,"text":"In my view, rather than treating this as a simple binary question of whether space tourism is good or bad, it is more productive to ask how the industry can be responsibly regulated — through environmental standards for launch providers and transparent safety regulations, for instance — given that the industry appears highly likely to continue growing regardless of any individual’s judgement about its underlying merit. Responsible regulation, rather than outright prohibition, which seems genuinely impractical at this stage, is probably the more realistic and constructive path forward."}]'::jsonb);

